  "confidence": 0.7,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "other",
      "components": ["R6", "R9"],
      "pins": ["R6.1", "R6.2", "R9.1", "R9.2", "J1.2", "J1.3"],
      "nets": ["FLOAT_HIGH", "FLOAT_LOW", "+3V3", "GND"],
      "observed": "On page 1 (WATER LEVEL SENSOR/SWITCHES) the 10 k pull resistors are tied to the wrong float nets: R6 (10k) pulls FLOAT_HIGH (J1.2 'HIGH') up to +3V3, and R9 (10k) pulls FLOAT_LOW (J1.3 'LOW') down to GND. These same nets also feed the hardware interlock transistors on page 3 (R2/FLOAT_LOW -> T1 base for the main pump, R11/FLOAT_HIGH -> T4 base for the ATO valve).",
      "expected": "The reference netlist/SPICE deck specifies the opposite assignment: 'R6 FLOAT_LOW +3V3 10k, R9 GND FLOAT_HIGH 10k' (spec §4.4), i.e. FLOAT_LOW pulled up and FLOAT_HIGH pulled down. With the float switches wired to GND (NC low-float, NO high-float), that gives the intended interlock: low water -> FLOAT_LOW high -> T1 on -> pump off; high water -> FLOAT_HIGH high -> T4 on -> valve off.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "Page 1, WATER LEVEL SENSOR/SWITCHES: R6 10k between +3V3 and the green FLOAT_HIGH line (J1 pin 2 HIGH); R9 10k between the green FLOAT_LOW line (J1 pin 3 LOW) and GND"},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "the .cir file: `R6 FLOAT_LOW +3V3 10k`, `R9 GND FLOAT_HIGH 10k`, `R2 FLOAT_LOW /BPUMP 4.7k`, `R11 FLOAT_HIGH /BATO 4.7k` (§4.4)"}
      ],
      "impact": "The pull-up/pull-down are swapped on both float channels, inverting the hardware safety interlocks. FLOAT_HIGH is held at +3V3 by default, keeping T4 conducting and the ATO valve gate pulled low, so the top-off valve stays closed when the tank is empty and opens only when the tank is full (overflow). FLOAT_LOW is held at GND, so T1 never turns on and the low-level pump cutoff is defeated. The board therefore behaves opposite to the intended ATO/pump-protection logic.",
      "confidence": 0.7
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The drawing matches the spec in power, isolation, I2C and stepper-driver topology, but the 10 k float-switch pull resistors R6/R9 are connected to the wrong nets (FLOAT_HIGH pulled up, FLOAT_LOW pulled down) versus the reference (R6 on FLOAT_LOW to +3V3, R9 on FLOAT_HIGH to GND), which inverts the ATO-valve and main-pump float interlocks."
}

